.TH "rfd" "1" "2026\-09\-13" "rushfind" "User Commands"
.PP
.SH NAME
.PP
rfd \- rushfind file finder with GNU\-find\-oriented syntax and parallel traversal
.PP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\fBrfd\fR [\fIglobal\-options\fR] [\fIpath\fR .\&.\&.\&] [\fIexpression\fR]
.PP
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBrfd\fR is the installed command for \fBrushfind\fR, a Rust implementation of Unix
\fBfind\fR(1) that keeps a GNU\-oriented command line while adding a parallel
traversal engine.\&
.PP
If no \fIpath\fR arguments are given, \fBrfd\fR searches the current directory.\& If no
\fIexpression\fR is given, \fBrfd\fR behaves as if \-print had been supplied.\&
.PP
Expression parsing follows GNU \fBfind\fR(1) precedence:
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
Adjacent primaries imply \-a.\&
.IP \(bu 4
\-a binds tighter than \-o.\&
.IP \(bu 4
, sequences expressions and preserves side effects.\&
.IP \(bu 4
Parentheses group subexpressions.\&
.PD
.PP
The default symlink mode is \-P.\&
.PP
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\fB\-P\fR, \fB\-H\fR, \fB\-L\fR
Control symlink handling.\& \-P is the default.\& \-H follows symlinks only for
command\-line roots.\& \-L follows symlinks during traversal.\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\fB\-\-help\fR
Print the built\-in quick reference and exit.\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\fB\-\-version\fR, \fB\-version\fR
Print build and target metadata and exit.\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\fB\-Olevel\fR
Accepted for GNU compatibility.\& The current implementation parses and records
the level but does not change optimizer behavior.\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\fB\-D\fR \fIopts\fR
Request debug categories.\& Use \-D help for the accepted category names.\&
Current debug output is a lightweight rushfind\-specific diagnostic stream, not
GNU findutils'\& detailed trace output.\&
.PD
.PP
.SH COMPATIBILITY OPTIONS
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\fB\-files0\-from\fR \fIfile\fR
Read NUL\-delimited start paths from \fIfile\fR.\& Use \- to read them from standard
input.\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\fB\-follow\fR
Accepted as a GNU positional compatibility option.\& In the current
implementation it effectively enables logical traversal.\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\fB\-warn\fR, \fB\-nowarn\fR
Control warnings for unknown \-D debug categories.\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\fB\-noleaf\fR
Accepted for GNU compatibility.\& It does not currently change traversal.\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\fB\-ignore_readdir_race\fR, \fB\-noignore_readdir_race\fR
Accepted and recorded for GNU compatibility.\& They do not currently change
runtime handling of disappearing entries.\&
.PD
.PP
\fBrfd\fR rejects explicit command\-line start paths together with \fB\-files0\-from\fR.\&
\fB\-files0\-from \-\fR also cannot be combined with \fB\-ok\fR or \fB\-okdir\fR, because both
would need standard input.\&
.PP
.SH TESTS AND ACTIONS
.PP
.SS Structural and boolean
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
(, ), !\&, \-not, \-a, \-and, \-o, \-or, ,
.IP \(bu 4
\-true, \-false
.IP \(bu 4
\-maxdepth, \-mindepth, \-depth, \-prune, \-xdev, \-mount, BSD \-s
.PD
.PP
BSD \-s sorts entries lexicographically within each directory and keeps the
result in ordered execution mode.\&
.PP
The \-xdev and \-mount tests are treated as traversal\-wide limits in the current
implementation rather than GNU'\&s positional controls.\&
.PP
.SS Name, path, and regex matching
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\-name, \-iname
.IP \(bu 4
\-path, \-ipath
.IP \(bu 4
\-wholename, \-iwholename
.IP \(bu 4
\-lname, \-ilname
.IP \(bu 4
\-regex, \-iregex
.IP \(bu 4
\-regextype
.IP \(bu 4
\-context
.PD
.PP
The \-regex and \-iregex tests match the whole displayed path.\& Supported
regextype values are emacs, posix\-extended, posix\-basic, rust, and pcre2.\&
.PP
The \-context predicate is recognized for GNU compatibility but reports an
unsupported diagnostic in this build.\&
.PP
.SS Type and filesystem matching
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\-type, \-xtype
.IP \(bu 4
\-fstype
.PD
.PP
The \-type and \-xtype tests accept GNU\-style comma lists such as \-type f,d.\& The
Solaris door type letter D is recognized for compatibility but reports an
unsupported diagnostic in this build.\&
.PP
.SS Identity and ownership matching
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\-samefile
.IP \(bu 4
\-inum
.IP \(bu 4
\-links
.IP \(bu 4
\-uid, \-gid
.IP \(bu 4
\-user, \-group
.IP \(bu 4
\-nouser, \-nogroup
.IP \(bu 4
Windows only: \-owner, \-owner\-sid, \-group\-sid
.PD
.PP
On Windows, \-uid and \-gid are not supported.\& Use \-owner\-sid and \-group\-sid
when raw SID matching is needed.\&
.PP
.SS Permissions, access, flags, and reparse points
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\-perm
.IP \(bu 4
\-readable, \-writable, \-executable
.IP \(bu 4
\-flags
.IP \(bu 4
Windows only: \-reparse\-type
.PD
.PP
Access predicates use kernel access checks and may intentionally differ from
the \-perm test.\&
.PP
Supported \-reparse\-type names are symbolic, mount\-point, app\-exec\-link,
wsl\-symlink, af\-unix, cloud, projfs, and other.\&
.PP
.SS Size and time matching
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\-size
.IP \(bu 4
\-empty
.IP \(bu 4
\-used
.IP \(bu 4
\-atime, \-ctime, \-mtime
.IP \(bu 4
\-amin, \-cmin, \-mmin
.IP \(bu 4
\-newer, \-anewer, \-cnewer
.IP \(bu 4
\-newerXY
.IP \(bu 4
\-daystart
.PD
.PP
Time predicates and \-used accept GNU\-style fractional magnitudes such as 0.\&5,
+1.\&25, and \-0.\&75.\&
.PP
The \-newerXY family supports read\-only literal reference forms in addition to
file
references:
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
@<unix\-seconds>[.\&frac]
.IP \(bu 4
YYYY\-MM\-DD
.IP \(bu 4
YYYY\-MM\-DD[ T]HH:MM[:SS][.\&frac][Z|+\-HH[:MM]]
.PD
.PP
Birth\-time forms depend on the active platform backend.\& Where the platform does
not expose the required data, planning fails with an explicit diagnostic.\&
.PP
.SS Output actions
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\-print
.IP \(bu 4
\-print0
.IP \(bu 4
\-printf
.IP \(bu 4
\-fprint
.IP \(bu 4
\-fprint0
.IP \(bu 4
\-fprintf
.IP \(bu 4
\-ls
.IP \(bu 4
\-fls
.PD
.PP
The current \-printf and \-fprintf subset supports GNU\-style field formatting
with %[flags][width][.\&precision]directive, including the space flag.\&
.PP
Supported directives currently include:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
%p %P %H %f %h %d %y %s %m %M %l %i %n %D %b %k %u %U %US %g %G %GS %F
%a %c %t %B %A* %C* %T* %B*
.fi
.RE
.PP
GNU special time selectors %A@, %C@, %T@, %B@, %A+, %C+, %T+, and %B+ are
supported.\& %A*, %C*, %T*, and %B* accept every GNU time selector, including
%e, %k, %l, %n, %s, %C, and %v; a selector with no dedicated rendering is
handed to the host strftime, which is what GNU find itself does, so
unrecognized selectors render exactly as GNU find renders them on the same
host.\&
.PP
An unrecognized directive warns on stderr once per occurrence in the format,
not once per visited entry, and is emitted literally instead of failing; the
exit status stays 0.\&
.PP
Escape handling also supports literal percent, backslash, newline, tab, and
NUL output forms.\&
.PP
.SS Exec, prompt, and mutation actions
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\-exec .\&.\&.\& ;
.IP \(bu 4
\-exec .\&.\&.\& +
.IP \(bu 4
\-execdir .\&.\&.\& ;
.IP \(bu 4
\-execdir .\&.\&.\& +
.IP \(bu 4
\-ok .\&.\&.\& ;
.IP \(bu 4
\-okdir .\&.\&.\& ;
.IP \(bu 4
\-delete
.IP \(bu 4
\-quit
.PD
.PP
The \-ok .\&.\&.\& + and \-okdir .\&.\&.\& + forms are not supported.\&
.PP
The \-execdir family checks \fBPATH\fR eagerly on non\-Windows hosts and rejects
empty or
non\-absolute entries as unsafe.\&
.PP
The \-delete action implies depth\-mode traversal, so directories are evaluated
after their
descendants.\&
.PP
.SH EXECUTION MODE
.PP
Use \fBRUSHFIND_WORKERS\fR to choose between ordered single\-worker execution and
the relaxed\-order parallel engine:
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
RUSHFIND_WORKERS=1 keeps traversal and output closest to GNU ordering.\&
.IP \(bu 4
Values greater than 1 enable the worker\-owned relaxed\-order parallel mode.\&
.IP \(bu 4
If unset, \fBrfd\fR uses host parallelism clamped to the range 1.\&.\&4.\&
.PD
.PP
In relaxed\-order parallel mode:
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
Side effects may be emitted out of sibling order.\&
.IP \(bu 4
\-fprint, \-fprint0, and \-fprintf writes are atomic per destination file.\&
.IP \(bu 4
\-ls stdout and \-fls writes are atomic per rendered record.\&
.IP \(bu 4
\-quit is cancellation: no new subtree work is published after it is observed,
but already granted work may still finish.\&
.IP \(bu 4
Depth\-mode actions preserve descendant\-before\-parent completion.\&
.PD
.PP
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\fBLC_ALL\fR, \fBLC_CTYPE\fR, \fBLANG\fR
Select the character encoding in that order.\& C and POSIX keep byte\-oriented
matching.\& UTF\-8 and supported legacy encodings use owned decoding tables
for glob and GNU regex predicates.\& Unknown encodings warn when matching or
presentation needs them, then fall back to byte\-oriented C/POSIX matching.\&
Collation ordering, equivalence classes, collating symbols, and
locale\-specific tailoring are not implemented.\& Case\-insensitive matching
uses single\-character folding only.\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\fBLC_MESSAGES\fR
May select localized \-ok and \-okdir prompt fragments.\& Prompt replies use a
built\-in ASCII affirmative parser; LC_CTYPE does not change confirmation
parsing.\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\fBRUSHFIND_WORKERS\fR
Controls worker count and execution mode.\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\fBRUSHFIND_WARNINGS\fR
Controls startup warnings.\& The default is on.\& Values 0, false, no, and off
suppress them.\&
.PD
.PP
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
0 if planning and execution complete without runtime errors or action
failures.\&
.IP \(bu 4
1 for parse failures, planning failures, runtime errors, or action failures.\&
.PD
.PP
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.SS Find Rust source files below src
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
rfd src \-type f \-name \&'*\&.rs\&'
.fi
.RE
.PP
.SS Skip target while printing regular files
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
rfd \&. \-path \&'\&./target/*\&' \-prune \-o \-type f \-print
.fi
.RE
.PP
.SS Use PCRE2 for whole\-path matching
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
rfd \&. \-regextype pcre2 \-regex \&'\&.*/(src|tests)/\&.*\\\&.rs\&' \-print
.fi
.RE
.PP
.SS Read search roots from standard input
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
printf \&'%s\\0\&' dir1 dir2 | rfd \-files0\-from \- \-maxdepth 1 \-type f
.fi
.RE
.PP
.SS Keep output close to GNU ordering
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
RUSHFIND_WORKERS=1 rfd \&. \-type f \-print
.fi
.RE
.PP
.SH NOTES
.PP
Linux is the reference platform.\& macOS, the BSDs, a generic Unix tier, and
native Windows are also supported, but some features are approximate or
platform\-specific.\&
.PP
On some generic Unix targets, \-fstype, %F, \-flags, and birth\-time predicates
fail during planning instead of running approximately.\&
.PP
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\fBfind\fR(1)
